= = = Sapphire-throated hummingbird = = =
The sapphire-throated hummingbird "Lepidopyga coeruleogularis" is a bright metallic-green hummingbird found in Panama, Colombia. More recently, it is seen in Costa Rica.
If a Man Answers is a 1962 American romantic comedy movie directed by Henry Levin and was based on the novel of the same name by Winifred Wolfe. It stars Bobby Darin, Sandra Dee, Micheline Presle, John Lund, Cesar Romero, Stefanie Powers, John Bleifer and was distributed by Universal Pictures.
= = = Jeremiah Clarke = = =
Jeremiah Clarke ( 1 December 1707) was an English baroque composer and organist. He is best known as the composer of the "Prince of Denmark's March", also called the "Trumpet Voluntary".
Life.
His early life is unknown. He was a singer at the Chapel Royal by 1685. He was an organist at Winchester College from 1692 to 1695. He became the vicar-choral of St Paul's Cathedral, London. On 15 May 1704, he became an organist at the Chapel Royal with William Croft. He shot himself on 1 December 1707.
Music.
Clarke wrote church music, theatre music, and harpsichord music. His most famous work is the "Prince of Denmark's March". It was first published as a harpsichord piece in "A Choice Collection of Ayres for the Harpsichord" in 1700. It was arranged by Sir Henry Wood in the 19th century for an orchestra. He mistakenly thought that Henry Purcell had composed it. It is often played in weddings and ceremonies.
Another famous piece, the "Trumpet Tune in D" may have also been written by Clarke. It was also mistakenly thought to have been composed by Henry Purcell. It is a movement from "The Island Princess", a set of incidental music. It was written together by Clarke and Daniel Purcell, Henry Purcell's younger brother.
= = = DJ Paul = = =
Paul Duane Beauregard (born January 12, 1975), better known by his stage name DJ Paul, is an American rapper, DJ, record producer, and actor from Memphis, Tennessee. He is a founding member of hip hop group Three 6 Mafia and an uncle of the late rapper Lord Infamous. He is also a part owner of FaZe Clan.
Discography.
Studio albums
= = = Bivolt = = =
Bárbara Bivolt (São Paulo, November 27, 1993) is a Brazilian R&amp;B singer, model and songwriter. Her hit singles "Pão Com Ovo" and "Olha Pra Mim" made her a household name in Brazil.
Biography and career.
At the age of 15, he joined in rhyming battles in São Paulo. He released the first debut album Bivolt which marked a new moment in his career before releasing the album where he wrote three of his own compositions "Lágrimas", "Entre Tu e Meu Som", "Olha Pra Mim". were important songs to win over the public. In July 2013, she started releasing footage from her live performances. In 2016, she received even more popularity when she recorded a song with fellow Brazilian rapper Cafuzo. In September 2018, he signed with Som Livre.
In 2020, she won the RAP TV National Award for Best Female Artist, the awards ceremony was broadcast virtually. In 2018, she was a guest on the TV show Cultura Manos e Minas where she performed singing the release of the song "Olha Pra Mim".
= = = Théo Ngwabidje Kasi = = =
Théo Ngwabidje Kasi (born 27 March 1971) is a politician from the Democratic Republic of the Congo who is Governor of South Kivu Province since 9 April 2019.
= = = Prince of Denmark's March = = =
The Prince of Denmark's March was written by Jeremiah Clarke. It is also called Trumpet Voluntary.
It was first published as a harpsichord piece in "A Choice Collection of Ayres for the Harpsichord" in 1700. It may have been dedicated to Prince George of Denmark and Anne of Great Britain's marriage. It was arranged by Sir Henry Wood for orchestra. He mistakenly thought that Henry Purcell had written the piece. It is often performed in weddings and other ceremonies.
= = = Independence Day (Indonesia) = = =
Independence Day is a national holiday in Indonesia. It is celebrated every 17 August. It commemorates the declaration of Indonesian independence on 17 August 1945.
= = = Dorsal consonant = = =
A dorsal consonant is a sound made with the back of your tongue. Dorsal sounds include palatal consonants, velar consonants and uvular consonants. For example, [k] and [j] are dorsal but [t] and [h] are not.
= = = Palatal click = = =
The palatal click is a sound used in some spoken languages. There are three different types of palatal click. They are tenuis, voiced, and nasal.
= = = Lateral consonant = = =
A lateral consonant is a sound where air flows out of the side of the mouth. English has one lateral, [l]. Languages have been found with lateral approximants, fricatives, affricates and flaps. Lateral trills are possible but no language has one.
= = = Open vowel = = =
An open vowel is a sound made without your tongue blocking anything. The International Phonetic Alphabet has four symbols for open vowels: a, ɶ, ɑ, and ɒ.
= = = Manner of articulation = = =
A manner of articulation is how a sound is made. For example, [s] is a fricative and [ŋ] is a nasal. The manner of articulation should not be confused with the place of articulation.
= = = Sergej Kraigher = = =
Sergej Kraigher (30 May 1914 – 17 January 2001) was a Yugoslav communist politician from Slovenia. He served as the President of Yugoslavia from 1981 to 1982. During World War II, he fought in the Yugoslav Resistance Movement.
= = = Ivan Maček = = =
Ivan Maček, nom de guerre Matija (28 May 1908 – 10 July 1993), was a Yugoslav Communist politician from Slovenia. He served as the President of the People's Assembly of SR Slovenia from 1963 to 1967.
Early life.
Maček was born in Spodnja Zadobrova near Ljubljana, Austria-Hungary (now in Slovenia). He became a member of the Communist Party of Yugoslavia in 1930. In 1935 was sent to the Soviet Union. He studied at the International Lenin School, Moscow. He returned to Yugoslavia in 1937. He became a member of the Central Committee of the Communist party of Slovenia. Yugoslav police detained him in 1938 and he was sentenced to four years in prison in Sremska Mitrovica.
Later life and death.
After the war, Maček moved into a mansion that had been confiscated from the pharmacist Leo Bahovec Maček was minister of the interior and vice-president of the Slovene government (1945–1953), vice-president of the Executive council of PR Slovenia and a member of the Federal Executive Council (1953–1963) and also the President of the People's Assembly of SR Slovenia (1963–1967). He was also a deputy in the National and Federal Assembly.
Maček died in Ljubljana, Slovenia on 10 July 1993 due to heart failure.
Awards.
Maček was declared as the People's Hero of Yugoslavia in 1952.
= = = Nina Garsoïan = = =
Nina G. Garsoïan (April 11, 1923 – August 14, 2022) was a French-born American historian. Her works focused on Armenian and Byzantine history. In 1969 she became the first female historian to get tenure at Columbia University. From 1977 to 1979, she was dean of the Graduate School of Princeton University. She was born in Paris, France.
Garsoïan died on August 14, 2022 in New York City at the age of 99.
= = = Roland Jefferson = = =
Roland Maurice Jefferson (born September 3, 1923) was an American botanist. He was the first African-American botanist to work for the United States National Arboretum. He worked there from 1956 to 1987.
He was a well known person when it came to flowering cherry trees. He wrote a 1977 book on how Washington, D.C. got its famous cherry trees.
= = = National Cherry Blossom Festival = = =
The National Cherry Blossom Festival is a spring celebration in Washington, D.C.. It celebrates the March 27, 1912, gift of Japanese cherry trees from Mayor Yukio Ozaki of Tokyo City to the city of Washington, D.C. Ozaki gave the trees to show the growing friendship between the United States and Japan.
The festival has large and colorful helium balloons, floats, marching bands along with music and other events.
= = = Prunus × yedoensis = = =
Prunus" × "yedoensis (synonym Cerasus" × "yedoensis) is a hybrid cherry tree between "Prunus speciosa" ("Oshima cherry") as father plant and "Prunus pendula" f. "ascendens" (syn. "Prunus itosakura", "Prunus subhirtella" var. "ascendens", "Edo higan") as mother. It is a hybrid born in Japan. It is is one of the most popular and widely planted cherry trees around the world today.
= = = Duggie Brown = = =
Duggie Brown (born Barry Dudley; 7 August 1940 – 16 August 2022) was an English comedian and actor. He was born in Rotherham, England. His career began in 1969. He played the Milkman in the movie "Kes" and George Freeman in the soap opera "Coronation Street".
Brown died on 16 August 2022, nine days after his 82nd birthday.
= = = Harriet Hageman = = =
Harriet M. Hageman (born October 18, 1962) is an American lawyer and Republican politician. She is the member of the U.S. House of Representatives representing Wyoming's at-large congressional district since 2023.
In August 2022, Hageman beat incumbent U.S. Representative Liz Cheney in a landslide victory. Her campaign was supported by former President Donald Trump. She was a candidate for Governor of Wyoming in the 2018 election.
= = = Fort Laramie, Wyoming = = =
Fort Laramie is a town in Goshen County, Wyoming, United States. The population was 206 at the 2020 census.
= = = La Grange, Wyoming = = =
LaGrange is a town in Goshen County, Wyoming, United States. The population was 372 at the 2020 census.
= = = Yoder, Wyoming = = =
Yoder is a town in Goshen County, Wyoming, United States. The population was 131 at the 2020 census.
= = = Copa Constitució = = =
The Copa Constitució is the cup competition in Andorran football. It started in 1991. The winner of the Copa is playing in the qualification for UEFA Europa League.
Mode.
The cup is played in five rounds. In the first round the eight best teams of the Segona Divisió play four matches. The winner must play against the under half of the table of the Primera Divisió . In the third round the best teams of the Primera Divisió start in the competition. Then a half- and a final is played.
Finals.
1992 and 1993 the cup was not held.
= = = Kal David = = =
David Raskin (June 15, 1943 – August 16, 2022), was an American blues guitarist and singer-songwriter. He was known as the voice of the "Sonny Eclipse" animatronic at Cosmic Ray's Starlight Cafe at Walt Disney World's Magic Kingdom.
Raskin died on August 16, 2022 at the age of 79.
= = = Narayan (writer) = = =
Narayan (26 September 1940 – 16 August 2022) was an Indian writer. He was best known for his debut novel "Kocharethi" (1998). He was born in Idukki, British Raj. He won the Kerala Sahitya Akademi Award in 1999.
Narayan died on 16 August 2022 in Idukki, India from COVID-19 at the age of 81.
= = = Hans Peterson = = =
Hans Peterson (26 October 1922 – 16 August 2022) was a Swedish children's writer. He was born in Väring, Sweden.
During his career, he was honored with the Svenska Dagbladet Literature Prize (1955), Nils Holgersson Plaque (1958), Deutscher Jugendliteraturpreis (1959) and Astrid Lindgren Prize (1971).
Peterson committed suicide on 16 August 2022 in Mölle, Sweden at the age of 99.
= = = Odd Reinsfelt = = =
Odd Reinsfelt (23 August 1941 – 16 August 2022) was a Norwegian Conservative Party politician and meteorologist. Reinsfelt was born in Bærum, Norway. He was the Mayor of Bærum from 1992 to 2011.
Reinsfelt died on 16 August 2022 in Bærum, one week before his 81st birthday.
= = = Brentwood, Los Angeles = = =
Brentwood is a suburban neighborhood in the Westside area of Los Angeles. It is a lightly populated residential district. The people are older, well-educated, and have high incomes.
= = = Kamini Kaushal = = =
Kamini Kaushal (born Uma Kashyap; 24 February 1927) is an Indian actress. She acted in "Neecha Nagar" (1946) and "Biraj Bahu" (1955), both of which won her acting awards. Recently, she acted in "Chennai Express" (2013) and the romantic drama "Kabir Singh" (2019).
= = = Kallistos (Ware) = = =
Kallistos Ware (born Timothy Richard Ware; 11 September 1934 – 24 August 2022) was an English bishop and theologian of the Eastern Orthodox Church. He became the titular bishopric of Diokleia in Phrygia () in 1982. He was later made a titular metropolitan bishopric in 2007. From 1966 to 2001, he was Spalding Lecturer of Eastern Orthodox Studies at the University of Oxford. Ware was born in Bath, Somerset, England.
On 14 August 2022, it was reported that Ware was in critical condition and "approaching the end of his life". He died ten days later on 24 August in Oxford, Oxfordshire at the age of 87.
= = = Retroflex consonant = = =
A retroflex consonant is a sound made with your tongue curled back. English has no retroflex consonants.
= = = Allophone = = =
An allophone is another way of saying a sound, usually influenced by nearby sounds or place in a word. For example, in English [ɫ] is an allophone of /l/ that appears in codas.
= = = Coda = = =
CODA or coda may mean:
= = = Coda (linguistics) = = =
A coda is made up of the consonants at the end of a syllable. For example, in the word ‘stands’ (IPA: /stændz/), /ndz/ is the coda. Many languages, such as Swahili, do not allow codas. A syllable without a coda is an open syllable, and one with a coda is a closed syllable.
= = = QU = = =
QU, Qu or qu may refer to:
= = = Institut des Hautes Études Scientifiques = = =
Institut des hautes études scientifiques, founded in 1958, is a higher education and research institution in France. It is located in Bures-sur-Yvette, close to Paris, in the University of Paris-Saclay campus.
= = = Bitter Springs Group = = =
Bitter Springs Group is a Precambrian fossil locality in Australia, which preserves microorganisms in silica. Bitter Springs type preservation ceased in the late Precambrian with the coming of silicifying organisms. Preserved fossils include cyanobacteria microfossils.
Bitter Springs is pretty much in the centre of Australia, in the south of the Northern Territory.
= = = Pseudacris sierra = = =
The seirra chorus frog, sierran tree frog, or eastern Oregon tree frog ("Pseudacris sierra") is a frog. It lives in the United States. It lives in California, Oregon, Montana, and Idaho. Scientists think it might also live in Utah.
= = = Kalikho Pul = = =
Kalikho Pul (20 July 1969 – 9 August 2016) was an Indian politician. He was Chief Minister of the north-eastern state of Arunachal Pradesh in India for a short-time in 2016.
On 9 August 2016, Pul allegedly committed suicide by hanging at his official residence in Itanagar. Pul's supporters protested in Itanagar demanding an investigation by the Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI).
= = = Gerald Grosvenor, 6th Duke of Westminster = = =
Major General Gerald Cavendish Grosvenor, 6th Duke of Westminster, (22 December 1951 – 9 August 2016), was a British landowner, businessman, philanthropist, Territorial Army general, and peer.
The Duke died on 9 August 2016 after suffering a heart attack.
= = = John Alan Robinson = = =
John Alan Robinson (9 March 1930 – 5 August 2016) was a British-American philosopher, mathematician, and computer scientist.
He was a professor emeritus at Syracuse University.
= = = Conor Leslie = = =
Conor Marie Leslie (born April 10, 1991) is an American actress. From 2015 to 2018, she also appeared in the first three seasons of the Amazon Prime Video alternate history thriller "The Man in the High Castle". She also played Donna Troy / Wonder Girl on the DC Universe / HBO Max superhero series "Titans" (2018–present).
= = = Erica Durance = = =
Erica Durance (; born June 21, 1978) is a Canadian actress. She is known for her role as Lois Lane in the WB/CW superhero television series "Smallville" and as Dr. Alex Reid in the CTV medical drama "Saving Hope".
= = = Hugh Grosvenor, 7th Duke of Westminster = = =
Hugh Richard Louis Grosvenor, 7th Duke of Westminster, was born 29 January 1991. He was styled as Earl Grosvenor until August 2016.
The Duke is a British aristocrat, billionaire, businessman, and owner of the Grosvenor Group. He became Duke of Westminster on 9 August 2016, on the death of his father Gerald, 6th Duke of Westminster.
The Duke and his family are 12th on the "Sunday Times Rich List" with an estimated net worth of £10.045 billion.
He was the world's richest person aged under 30.
= = = Steve LaTourette = = =
Steven Clare LaTourette (July 22, 1954 – August 3, 2016) was an American politician who served as the U.S. Representative for Ohio's 19th congressional district and then Ohio's 14th congressional district from 1995 to 2013.
= = = Articulator = = =
An articulator is a part of the body used to make a sound. For example, in [f], the top teeth and bottom lip are articulators.
= = = Lee Ji-ah = = =
Kim Sang-eun (; born August 6, 1978), known professionally as Lee Ji-ah, is an actress from South Korea. She is famous for her roles in the television series " The Legend" (2007), "Beethoven Virus" (2008), ' (2010), "Me Too, Flower!" (2011), "Thrice Married Woman" (2013), "My Mister" (2018) and ' (2020–2021).
= = = Ganondagan State Historic Site = = =
Ganondagan State Historic Site or Boughton Hill is a Native American historic site in Ontario County, New York. The site has two main sections. The first section now includes burials and a large bark longhouse. It is a National Historic Site. The other section includes fortified storage for grains at Fort Hill. This section is a National Register of Historic Places. There are also several trails. Ganondagan was the largest village of the Seneca people. There were about 150 longhouses in this village. The city was part of the western gateway of the Iroquois Confederacy. The town Ganondagan is called the "Town of Peace". There is a burial site of Jigonhsasee. She was one of the founders of the Iroquois Confederacy. Marquis de Denonville destroyed the town in 1687.
= = = Genesee River = = =
The Genesee River is a river of Lake Ontario. It flows northward through Twin Tiers of New York and Pennsylvania.
= = = Marquis de Denonville = = =
Jacques-René de Brisay de Denonville, Marquis de Denonville (10 December 1637 – 22 September 1710) was a governor of New France. He was important during the Beaver Wars. He arrived in the colonies when there was much conflict between the Irouquois and the English. He led an attack against the Seneca people in 1687. He destroyed many towns.
= = = Discovery Family (French TV channel) = = =
Disovery Family was a French family-oriented television channel. It was owned by Discovery, Inc. (now part of Warner Bros. Discovery). It was launched on 14 September 2017. The channel was available only for SFR TV subscribers, as a part of a deal between SFR and Discovery.
Unlike Discovery Family, the channel did not include programming aimed at children.
= = = Classical Marxism = = =
Classical Marxism refers to the economic, philosophical and sociological theories by Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels. It was contrasted with later developments in Marxism, especially Marxism–Leninism.
= = = Sadhguru = = =
Jagadish "Jaggi" Vasudev (born 3 September 1957), more commonly called Sadhguru, is an Indian yoga guru.
= = = Second Treaty of Buffalo Creek = = =
The Second Treaty of Buffalo Creek, also known as the Treaty with the New York Indians, 1838 was a treaty between Native Americans and the United States. The Natives in the treaty were the Seneca, Mohawk, Cayuga , Oneida , Onondaga and Tuscarora. New York Indians gave up lands. The Seneca would give up (Buffalo Creek Reservation, Tonawanda Reservation, Oil Springs Reservation, Cattaraugus Reservation, and Allegany Reservation for reservations in Kansas and Missouri. The third and fourth Treaties of Buffalo's Creek were just changes of the second treaty.
= = = Battle of Minisink = = =
The Battle of Minisink was a battle of the American Revolutionary War. The British and Iroquois Confederacy defeated the Americans. The Americans were not prepared and had little experience. The battle was at Minisink Ford, New York, on July 22, 1779. It was the only major fight in the northern Delaware Valley.
= = = Jigonhsasee = = =
Jigonhsasee (alternately spelled Jikonhsaseh and Jikonsase) was one of the founders of the Iroquois Confederacy. The other founders were the Great Peacemaker and Hiawatha. She is known as the Mother of Nations. According to legend, the Peacemaker came to Jigonhsasee. He told her about his mission to build a confederacy. That led to the founding of the Iroquois confederacy which took place around 900 CE.
= = = Charlie Chan in Shanghai = = =
Charlie Chan in Shanghai is a 1935 American mystery movie directed by James Tinling and is the 12th movie in the Charlie Chan series. It stars Warner Oland, Irene Hervey, Russell Hicks, Keye Luke, Jon Hall, Halliwell Hobbes and was distributed by 20th Century Fox.
= = = Requiem (Michael Haydn) = = =
Michael Haydn's Requiem (full title Missa pro defuncto Archiepiscopo Sigismondo) was written after the death of Archbishop Sigismund von Schrattenbach of Salzburg in December 1771. It was first performed in January 1772. Leopold Mozart and his son Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart were among the musicians performing. It inspired Mozart's own Requiem, which was written in 1791.
Structure.
The requiem is written for SATB choir and soloists, two bassoons, four trumpets, three trombones, timpani, strings, and basso continuo. The requiem is divided into five sections:
= = = CE Principat = = =
Club Esportiu Principat was an Andorran football club from the city of Andorra la Vella. The club was founded in 1989. He won three times the Andorran Premier League and five times the Andorran Cup. The club was dissolved in 2015.
History.
The club was founded in 1989 by "Penya Madridista Restaurant Charlie's" (Real Madrid Supporters' Club of Charlie's Restaurant) as Club Esportiu Principat. The club had three consecutives league victories between 1997 and 1999 and five consecutives cup trophies between 1995 and 1999. The club was founding member of the Andorran Premier League in 1995.
In 1997 the club was the first Andorran club in an European competition playing versus Dundee United from Scotland. The Andorran team pülayed three times in the (UEFA Cup) but lost in the first round each time.
The senior club was registered in Segona Divisió 2015 but withdrew from the competition in the first round. Lower and youth teams continued to compete in their competitions. In 2015 the club was dissolved.
Colours and badge.
The team's traditional color was purple. In the beginnings till the mid-2000s the first kit was white due to the origins of the club linked with Real Madrid supporters in Andorra.
Numb Chucks is an animated television series that was created by Phil LaFrance and Jamie LeClaire and premiered on YTV in Canada on January 7, 2014, and it premiered on Boomerang in the United States on January 12, 2015. The series was focused on Dilweed and Fungus, two woodchucks with big hearts, who are obsessed with using their bungling kung-fu skills to protect the lives of the citizens in their town of Ding-a-Ling Springs. The series was cancelled, and its final episode aired on December 1, 2016, after a total of 52 episodes were produced.
= = = Constel·lació Esportiva = = =
Constel·lació Esportiva was an Andorran football club from Andorra La Vella. They started in the Andorran First Division (Campionat de Lliga) in 1998. 2000 they became champion.
In this year the Football Federation accused the team of trying to buy players from other teams and they were financial irregularities and the team did not want to divide its winnings from the UEFA cup with the league. So they were kicked out of the First Division for seven years.
= = = Andorra women's national football team = = =
Andorra women's national football team is a national football team from Andorra. They are yet to feature at the FIFA Women's World Cup.
= = = Butch Thompson = = =
Richard Enos Thompson, universally known as "Butch Thompson" (November 28, 1943 – August 14, 2022) was an American jazz pianist and clarinetist. He was best known for his ragtime and stride works. From 1974 to 1986, he appeared on the radio show "A Prairie Home Companion". From the 1960s until 2021, he led the Butch Thompson Trio.
Thompson died on August 14, 2022 in Saint Paul, Minnesota at the age of 78.
= = = Hanae Mori = = =
was a Japanese fashion designer. She was the first Asian woman to be honored as an official "haute couture" design house by the Fédération française de la couture in France. She was honored with the Order of Culture (Japan) in 1996.
Mori died on 11 August 2022 at her home in Tokyo, Japan from cardiopulmonary arrest at the age of 96.
= = = Paul X. Rinn = = =
Paul Xavier Rinn (13 September 1946 – 3 August 2022) was an officer in the United States Navy. He was the first commanding officer of the "USS Samuel B. Roberts (FFG-58)" and was in charge when the ship struck a mine in the Persian Gulf on 14 April 1988. Rinn was born in New York City. He was awarded a Purple Heart and the Legion of Merit for his actions on "USS Samuel B. Roberts".
Rinn died on 3 August 2022 from a heart attack while at a speaking event in Boston, Massachusetts at the age of 75.
= = = Legion of Merit = = =
The Legion of Merit (LOM) is a military award of the United States Armed Forces. It is given for great outstanding services and achievements. The decoration is given to members of the eight uniformed services of the United States as well as to military and political figures of foreign governments.
= = = Viktor Zozulin = = =
Viktor Viktorovich Zozulin (; 10 October 1944 – 16 August 2022) was a Russian actor. He was born in Moscow, then-Soviet Union. He was known for his roles in "Operation Y and Shurik's Other Adventures", "I Loved You" and "King Stag". His career began in 1965.
Zozulin died on 16 August 2022 in Moscow from a long-illness at the age of 77.
= = = Toby Curtis = = =
Sir Noble Thomson "Toby" Curtis (13 November 1939 – 17 August 2022) was a New Zealand educator and Māori leader. He was director of primary teacher education at Auckland College of Education and then dean of the education faculty at Auckland Institute of Technology during the 1990s, becoming deputy vice chancellor at Auckland University of Technology in 2000.
Curtis died at his home on Lake Rotoiti, New Zealand on 17 August 2022, at the age of 82.
= = = Modern Romance = = =
Modern Romance is a 1981 romantic comedy directed by Albert Brooks (who also stars) and also starring James L. Brooks, Bruno Kirby, Kathryn Harrold, George Kennedy, Albert Henderson. It was distributed by Columbia Pictures.
= = = Diego Uribe Vargas = = =
Diego Uribe Vargas (1 November 1931 – 12 August 2022) was a Colombian politician, lawyer and academic. He was a member of the Colombian Liberal Party. He was Minister of Foreign Affairs from 1978 to 1981. From 1974 to 1990, he was a Senator. He was also the Governor of Cundinamarca from 1970 to 1972. From 1972 to 1974, he was a member of the Chamber of Representatives.
Uribe Vargas was born in Bogotá, Colombia. Before his death, he was a professor at the National University of Colombia.
Uribe Vargas died in Bogotá on 12 August 2022 at the age of 90.
= = = Ministry of Foreign Affairs (Colombia) = = =
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs (MRE) () also known as the Chancellery (), is the Cabinet position of the Government of Colombia responsible for the international relations of Colombia and the country's foreign policy.
= = = Julio Londoño Paredes = = =
Julio Londoño Paredes (born 10 July 1938) is a retired Colombian Army Lieutenant Colonel, writer and politician. He was born in Bogotá, Colombia. He has worked under the administrations of six presidents from 1983 to 2010: Belisario Betancur, Virgilio Barco Vargas, César Gaviria, Ernesto Samper, Andrés Pastrana Arango and Álvaro Uribe.
Londoño Paredes was the Minister of Foreign Affairs from 1986 to 1990. He also was the Permanent Representative to the Organization of American States from 1990 to 1994 and to the United Nations from 1994 to 1998. Londoño Paredes also was an Ambassador to Panama from 1983 to 1986 and to Cuba from 1998 until 2010.
= = = Farges-en-Septaine = = =
Farges-en-Septaine is a commune in Cher, Centre-Val de Loire, France. About 999 people lived there in January 2019.
= = = Crézançay-sur-Cher = = =
Crézançay-sur-Cher is a commune in Cher, Centre-Val de Loire, France. About 59 people lived there in January 2019.
= = = Daniel Quintero = = =
Daniel Quintero Calle (born 26 July 1980) is a Colombian politician. He was the mayor of Medellín since 2020. Quintero was suspended as mayor from 10 May to 21 June 2022 for trying to interfere in the 2022 presidential election.
= = = Charentonnay = = =
